These run script templates are dedicated to code for running the SCHF calculation associated with
the mean-field description of the NNN Coulomb repulsion, resulting in a Haldane order.

General conventions:
pval is the {p, 3} appearing in the Schlafli symbol for hyperbolic lattices (q assumed to be = 3)
nval is the number of generations (unlike paper in code we start counting from 1 not 0)
alpha_NH is the alpha appearing in the NH construction, controlling the strength of the NH
small_disorder_strength is the small disorder added to stabilize the NH eigensolver
coulomb_V2 is the V2 parameter which controls the strength of the NNN Coulomb repulsion
qaxial is the parameter that controls the strength of the axial magnetic field
(note that to match qaxial values quoted in paper, you must enter the sqrt of that value in the code for qaxial
for instance if in the paper we say q=0.0016, in the code you need to use qaxial=0.04)

schf_tolerance is the tolerance amount before SCHF computation deems the simulation as converged
*Note for the convergence of the SCHF computation the tolerance value (currently set to 0.001 in the script)
will likely need to be loosened*

final_delta_HO is the final Haldane order, order parameter (\delta_{\rm HO})

eta_values returned from SCHF calculation is a dictionary with keys 'V2={}'. For a given V2 value the data sturcture
is a 3xN array where the 0th and 1st rows correspond to the row and column index of the 2nd row eta value.

total_mag_flux describes the total magnetic flux through the system for each iteration fo the SCHF computation.
To check that total magnetic flux is zero after averaging between both directions of the axial field,
get total_mag_flux from both configurations and add them.

max_real_comp is the maximum real component of the eta values during each iteration of the SCHF computation. While we
delete any real component of eta before forming the MFT interaction term for the next iteration, we check that the
real component is not becoming too large over the course of the simulation in order to justify throwing it out

smallest_real_ev is the minimum real component of the eigenvalues for each iteration
imag_ev_bounds is the extremal magnitudes of the imaginary component of the eigenvalues for each iteration
Can take ratio of min_real_comp_ev to imag_ev_bounds to check that imaginary component stays sufficiently small

Examples of the above checks are shown in the run templates

